hadiths[6]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1035","Narrated to us Abdul Jabbar, narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Ibn Abi Labid, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abu Hurairah, H, and Muhammad bin Abd al-Ala narrated to us Al-San’ani, we are Bishr, meaning Ibn Al-Mufaddal, Ibn Aoun told us, on the authority of Muhammad Ibn Sirin, on the authority of Abu Hurairah, he said: Abu Al-Qasim led us in prayer. May God bless him and grant him peace. Bundar told us, Muadh bin Muadh told us, Ibn Aoun told us, on the authority of Muhammad, he said: Abu Hurairah said. H, Bundar told us, Hussein, meaning Ibn Al-Hasan, told us, Ibn Aoun told us, H, Bundar told us, Ibn Abi Adiy told us, he said: Ibn Aoun narrated to us, on the authority of Muhammad, on the authority of Abu Hurairah, on the authority of Saeed bin Abdul Rahman, on the authority of Sufyan, on the authority of Ayoub, on the authority of Ibn Sirin, on the authority of Abu Hurairah, on the authority of Ya’qub al-Dawraqi, on the authority of Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal, on the authority of Salamah, and he is Ibn Alqamah, on the authority of Muhammad On the authority of Abu Hurairah, he said: “The Messenger of God, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, led with us one of the evening prayers. He prayed two rak’ahs, then gave the salutation, then he brought a piece of wood.” It was displayed in the mosque, and he said with his hands on it, as if he was angry. He said: The two speedsters came out from the doors of the mosque, and they said: The prayer has been shortened, and in The people were Abu Bakr and Omar, so they were afraid to speak to him, and among the people was a man who had long hands, so he was called Dhul-Yadin, so he said: O Messenger of God. Did you forget or was the prayer shortened? He said: “I did not forget and the prayer was not shortened.” He said: “Is it as those with two hands say?” They said: “Yes.” He said: “So he came and prayed whatever it was.” He left, then he said salaam, then he said “Allahu Akbar” and prostrated like his prostration or longer, then he raised his head, then he said “Allahu Akbar” and prostrated like his prostration or longer, then he raised his head. He shook his head and said “Allahu Akbar,” and he said: “And perhaps they said to him: ‘Then he salaam.’” So he says: “I was informed that Imran bin Husayn said: Then he salaam.” This is the hadith of Al-San’ani."
"1036","Issa bin Ibrahim Al-Ghafiqi told us, Ibn Wahb told us, Amr bin Al-Harith told me, Qatada bin Du’amah told me, on the authority of Ibn Sirin On the authority of Abu Hurairah, on the authority of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, the same, meaning that he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness on the day Dhu Al-Din came to him after Delivery. Abu Bakr said: The narration of Ibn Sirin on the authority of Abu Hurairah indicates the omission of those who claimed that this story occurred before the prohibition of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. peace and blessings of God be upon him, from speech during prayer, and whoever understands knowledge, and contemplates the narrations of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, and the words of the narrators of this narration, will know. This saying is ignorance on the part of whoever said it in the report of Ibn Sirin, on the authority of Abu Hurairah, the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, led us in prayer, and this is how he narrated it. Malik bin Anas, on the authority of Dawud bin Al-Husayn, on the authority of Abu Sufyan, the client of Banu Ahmad, on the authority of Abu Hurairah, he said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, prayed for us Peace be upon him"
"1037","Yunus bin Abd al-Ala al-Sadfi told us, Ibn Wahb told us that Malik told them, on the authority of Dawud bin al-Husayn, on the authority of Abu Sufyan A servant of Banu Abi Ahmad, who said: I heard Abu Hurairah say: “The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, prayed for us the afternoon prayer, and he gave the salutation in two rak’ahs. Then Dhu al-Yadin stood up and said: Did you shorten the prayer, O Messenger of God, or did you forget? Then the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: “All of that did not happen,” and he said: Some of that happened, O Messenger of God. Then the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, approached the people and said: “Did the one with two hands give the truth?” They said: Yes. So the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, completed what remained of the prayer, then he prostrated two prostrations while sitting after the salutation.”"
"1038","Abu Bakr said: And this is how it was narrated by Aban bin Yazid Al-Attar, on the authority of Yahya bin Abi Kathir, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abu Hurairah, The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, prayed with them and mentioned the story. Muhammad bin Yahya told us, Muslim bin Ibrahim told us, Aban bin Yazid told us, Abu Bakr said: Abu Hurairah reports that he witnessed This is the prayer with the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, which contains this story. So how could this story of Dhu al-Yadin occur before the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, prohibited it? May God bless him and grant him peace, about speaking during prayer? Ibn Masoud narrates that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, informed him when he returned from the land of Abyssinia when he greeted the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, Among the things that God caused was that they should not speak during prayer, and the return of Ibn Masoud from the land of Abyssinia occurred before the Battle of Badr, as Ibn Masoud had He had witnessed Badr, and claimed that he had killed Abu Jahl ibn Hisham on that day. I dictated this story in the book of jihad, and Abu Hurairah He only came to Medina years after Badr. He came to Medina."
"1039","The Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, was in Khaybar, and he appointed Siba’ ibn ‘Urfatah al-Ghafari as his successor in charge of the city."
"1040","**
Narrated to us Abu 'Ammar, who said: Narrated to us al-Fadl ibn Musa, who said: Narrated to us Khuthaym ibn 'Irak ibn Malik, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:

""I came to al-Madinah while the Prophet (ﷺ) was at Khaybar, and Siba' ibn 'Urfutah had been appointed as the governor of al-Madinah.""

I have already mentioned this report elsewhere. I have also mentioned his arrival to the Prophet (ﷺ) at Khaybar in the Book of Jihad.

Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid said, on the authority of Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who heard Abu Hurayrah (RA) say:

""I accompanied the Prophet (ﷺ) for three years.""

This was narrated by Bundar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, on the authority of Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid.

Abu Hurayrah (RA) only accompanied the Prophet (ﷺ) at Khaybar and thereafter. He reports that he witnessed this prayer with the Prophet (ﷺ). So whoever claims that the report of Ibn Mas'ud (RA) abrogates the story of Dhu al-Yadayn, if he were to reflect upon knowledge and abandon stubbornness and not oppose his intellect, he would know the impossibility of this claim. For it is inconceivable that the later report should be abrogated while the earlier one abrogates, and the story of Dhu al-Yadayn occurred years after the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade speaking in prayer. How then can the later report be abrogated while the earlier one abrogates?

Moreover, the story of Dhu al-Yadayn is not connected to the Prophet's (ﷺ) prohibition of speaking in prayer. It is not of that category. For speaking in prayer deliberately while knowing one is in prayer was permissible, and then it was abrogated, and they were forbidden from deliberately speaking in prayer after it had been permitted to them previously. It was not that they were permitted to speak in prayer forgetfully or unknowingly, not knowing they were in prayer, and then that was abrogated.

Can a person of sound intellect, who understands the least bit of knowledge, say that Allah reprimanded a person for speaking while not knowing he was in prayer? Or say that Allah forbade a person from speaking in prayer while he did not know that Allah had forbidden speaking in prayer? Rather, it is obligatory upon a person not to speak in prayer after he knows that speaking in prayer is prohibited and not permissible.

Mu'awiyah ibn al-Hakam al-Sulami (RA) only spoke while not knowing that speaking in prayer was prohibited. He said in prayer behind the Prophet (ﷺ), when he responded to a sneeze and the people stared at him, ""May my mother lose me! Why do you stare at me?"" When he spoke in prayer with these words, not knowing that this speech was prohibited in prayer, the Prophet (ﷺ) taught him that people's speech in prayer is prohibited and not permissible. He did not command him (RA) to repeat the prayer in which he had spoken.

In the story of Dhu al-Yadayn, the Prophet (ﷺ) only spoke regarding something outside of prayer, and that he had completed the obligatory prayer in its entirety. Dhu al-Yadayn spoke to the Prophet (ﷺ) while not knowing that some of the obligatory prayer remained. For he thought it possible that the prayer had reverted to the initial two rak'ahs, as it was at the beginning. Do you not hear him say to the Prophet (ﷺ): ""Was the prayer shortened, or did you forget?""

The Prophet (ﷺ) answered him that he had not forgotten nor was the prayer shortened. At that moment, he was not certain that some of that prayer remained. So he sought confirmation from his companions and said to them: ""Is it as Dhu al-Yadayn says?"" When he was certain that two rak'ahs remained of that prayer, he made them up. The Prophet (ﷺ) did not speak in this incident after he knew and was certain that some of that prayer remained.

As for his companions who answered him and said to the Prophet (ﷺ) after asking him: ""Is it as Dhu al-Yadayn says?"" they said: ""Yes."" This was the required response they should have given. Even though they were in prayer, knowing and certain that they were in the obligatory prayer itself, Allah, the Exalted, distinguished His chosen Prophet (ﷺ) from the rest of his nation with His honor and favor by obligating those who pray to answer him, even if they were in prayer, in His saying: ""O you who believe, respond to Allah and the Messenger when he calls you to what gives you life."" (Surah al-Anfal, 8:24)

The Prophet (ﷺ) said to Ubayy ibn Ka'b (RA) and Abu Sa'id ibn al-Mu'alla (RA), when each of them called him individually while he was in prayer and he did not answer until he finished the prayer: ""Did you not hear what was revealed to me, or words to that effect: 'O you who believe, respond to Allah and the Messenger when he calls you to what gives you life.'"" (Surah al-Anfal, 8:24)

I have mentioned these two reports elsewhere.

There is a difference between the companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) in their speech on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn and the speech of Dhu al-Yadayn himself in some rulings.

As for the speech of Dhu al-Yadayn at the beginning, it is not permissible for anyone after the Prophet (ﷺ) to speak like Dhu al-Yadayn did. For every person who prays after the Prophet (ﷺ), when he gives the salam after two rak'ahs of Zuhr or 'Asr, knows and is certain that two rak'ahs remain of his prayer, for revelation has ceased after the Prophet (ﷺ). It is inconceivable that the obligatory prayer should be reduced after the Prophet (ﷺ). So every speaker who knows that the obligatory prayer of Zuhr and 'Asr is four rak'ahs each, when he speaks after having prayed two rak'ahs with two remaining, knows with certainty that his speech is prohibited for him, forbidden, and that he is speaking before completing the obligatory prayer.

Dhu al-Yadayn, when the Prophet (ﷺ) gave the salam after two rak'ahs, did not know nor was he certain that some of the prayer remained. Nor did he know that speech was prohibited for him, for at that time it was possible in his view that the obligatory prayer had reverted to the initial two rak'ahs, as it was at the beginning. His words to the Prophet (ﷺ) indicate this: ""Was the prayer shortened, or did you forget?""

I have explained the reason for which the companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) spoke after the Prophet's (ﷺ) saying to Dhu al-Yadayn: ""I did not forget, nor was the prayer shortened,"" and I have made clear that their obligation was to answer the Prophet (ﷺ), even if they were in prayer. This obligation today is dropped; it is not permissible for a Muslim to answer anyone with speech while he is in prayer.

So whoever speaks after the cessation of revelation and says to a person who has given the salam after two rak'ahs: ""Was the prayer shortened, or did you forget?"" it is obligatory upon him to repeat that prayer if he knows that the obligatory prayer is four rak'ahs, not two.

Likewise, it is obligatory upon whoever speaks while certain that he has not completed the obligatory prayer in its entirety, whether he speaks before giving the salam after two rak'ahs or after giving the salam after two rak'ahs.

Similarly, it is obligatory upon whoever answers a person while in prayer to repeat that prayer, for Allah, the Exalted, has not made it permissible for any human to answer anyone in prayer except the Prophet (ﷺ), whom Allah has distinguished with this.

This is a lengthy matter. I have mentioned it at length along with the objections of some who opposed our companions in this matter, and I clarify the absurdity of what they argued against our companions in this matter, from the impossible to what resembles nonsense, if Allah grants us success.""

---

**
Narrated to us Abu 'Ammar, who said: Narrated to us al-Fadl ibn Musa, who said: Narrated to us Khuthaym ibn 'Irak ibn Malik, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:

""I accompanied the Prophet (ﷺ) for three years.""

This was narrated by Bundar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, on the authority of Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid."
